ueue with negative depth, we can simply push (-depth, id, node_id). This will maintain order.\n\nBut we also need to handle the case where a node that was previously removed is still in queue. We skip it. So it's fine.\n\nNow, the output: retained_node_ids: list of ids of nodes that were not removed at the end. visit_order: list of ids in order they were popped from queue (including repeats? but if we push the same node again, it will be popped again later, so it appears multiple times. Is that intended? The spec says \"complete visit order\". Possibly they mean the order in which nodes are considered, including reconsiderations. I think yes.\n\nBut let's test with the first fixture case to see if it makes sense.\n\nFirst fixture payload:\nnodes:\n- root: depth0, removable false, utility 1.0\n- n1: depth1, parent root, removable false, utility 0.9414\n- n2: depth1, parent root, removable true, utility 0.7422\n- n3: depth1, parent root, removable false, utility 0.7601\n- n4: depth2, parent n2, removable true, utility 0.2087\n- n5: depth2, parent n3, removable false, utility 0.0216\n- n6: depth2, parent n1, removable true, utility 0.3524\nthreshold = 0.55\n\nInitial queue sorted descending depth, id ascending:\ndepth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (sorted: n4, n5, n6)\ndepth1: n1, n2, n3\ndepth0: root\nSo queue = [n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root]\n\nNow process:\npop n4: leaf? children of n4? originally none, and no children added. Yes leaf. removable True, utility 0.2087 <= 0.55 → remove. visit order: [n4]. parent n2 (still present), enqueue n2 (push to queue).\nQueue now: n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root, and newly added n2 (will be after root in heap? Since depth1, -depth=-1, n2 id \"n2\", so key (-1, \"n2\"). Existing n2 already in queue? The original n2 is still in queue at position after n1? Actually queue currently has n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root, and we push n2 again. So there will be duplicate n2. That's okay.\n\npop n5: leaf? children none? Yes. removable false -> cannot remove. visi