ble and had child n5 (still present). root retained.\n\nNow verify if this matches expected behavior. The algorithm seems sound.\n\nBut is there any nuance about \"leaf\"? A leaf is a node with no children that are still present (i.e., not removed). So after removal, we need to dynamically check if children present. We'll maintain a set of removed ids.\n\nNow regarding queue initialization: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" This is a one-time initialization. We use heapq to maintain sorted order.\n\nPotential issues: When we enqueue parent, we must ensure that we push it at the correct position. With heap, always push with same key. Works.\n\nNow about retaining the order of stable node ID: We need to define stable order. I'll use the node's id as a string for sorting. Since the example IDs are \"root\", \"n1\", etc., string sorting is fine.\n\nNow produce the Python code for solve(case).\n\nEdge cases: Node may have parent that is None (root). parent field is null in JSON. In Python, it's None. We should handle that.\n\nAlso, we need to use only stdlib. Use heapq.\n\nNow final output: JSON with implementation key containing Python source. Must escape.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    thresh = case['utility_threshold']\n    # Build dicts\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: set() for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].add(n['id'])\n    removed = set()\n    # Initialize priority queue with all nodes (depth descending, id ascending)\n    import heapq\n    heap = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        # key: (-depth, id)\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id'], n['id']))\n    visit_order = []\n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, node_id, _ = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if node_id in removed:\n            # already removed, skip but still record? The spec says skip nodes already removed; should we record? The visit order should include all popped items? It's ambiguous. I think we should record only when we process (including skip). Because we popped it; we considered it. So record.\n            visit_order.append(node_id)\n            continue\n        node = node_map[node_id]\n        # Determine if leaf: no children that are still present\n        is_leaf = all(child in removed for child in children[node_id])\n        if is_leaf and node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= thresh:\n            # Remove\n            removed.add(node_id)\n            # Enqueue parent\n            parent = node['parent']\n            if parent is not None and parent not in removed:\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-node_map[parent]['depth'], parent, parent))\n        visit_order.append(node_id)\n    retained = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\n\nBut note: we appended visit_order inside if-else for both cases. But careful: we append after processing. For the case where we remove, we append after removal. That's fine. For the skip case (already removed), we append and then continue. So each pop results in one entry in visit_order.\n\nHowever, consider if we push a duplicate node and later pop it when it is already removed (because it was removed by a previous processing of another duplicate). That will be caught by the check at top. That's fine.\n\nNow verify with first case: we expected visit_order length 10. Let's simulate quickly: initial heap has 7 items. Removal of n4 and n6 will push n2 and n1 once each, so total pushes = 7+2=9 pops? Actually we pushed 2 times, so total heap size increases by 2. We pop 9 times? Then visit_order length 9? But we also had duplicates? Let's recalc: initial 7 items = 7 pops. After removing n4, we push n2 => heap now has 6 items (since we popped one) +1 =7. After removing n6, we push n1 => heap now has 7 items. So total pops = 7 (initial) +